function e(a){return`Inside this sector there are ${a} industries. Let's understand them one by one.`}const r={kicker:"KNOW YOUR SECTOR",headline:"Industrials",tagline:"The machines, metals, and wires behind every capex cycle",overview:["Industrials is the machinery, components, and infrastructure that keeps every other sector physically running. Cables, transformers, switchgear, cranes, boilers, turbines, forged parts, packaging — none of them make headlines on their own, but they sit at the heart of every capex cycle, every factory expansion, every new power line, and every real-estate build.","The sector is inherently cyclical. It booms when governments, utilities, and private businesses spend on new capacity, and it cools when capex pauses. In India specifically, Industrials has benefitted from a decade-long push into domestic manufacturing, power transmission upgrades, renewable energy buildout, and infrastructure investment — making it one of the most keenly watched sectors for structural momentum.",e(6)],industries:[{name:"Electrical Equipment",oneLiner:"The electrical backbone — cables, switchgear, transformers, motors, meters.",details:'These are the companies that make the "electrical plumbing" of every building, factory, and power grid. Switches, circuit breakers, transformers that step voltage up or down, electric motors, smart electricity meters, industrial automation panels. Every time a new home, mall, factory, metro line, or substation is built, someone has to supply these. Demand rides on real-estate construction, power transmission capex, smart-meter rollouts, and factory automation. Margins are more stable than pure engineering because these are largely catalogue products — you order what you need off a list.',examples:["ABB","SIEMENS","CGPOWER","THERMAX","POWERINDIA","WAAREEENER","APARINDS","SCHNEIDER"]},{name:"Engineering",oneLiner:"Turnkey project companies — design, build, and execute complete industrial systems end-to-end.",details:`These are the "we'll build the whole thing for you" companies. If a refinery, power plant, metro project, or huge factory needs to be built, these firms handle design, procurement, construction, and commissioning as a single package. They live off large orders (often hundreds of crores each) that take 2-4 years to execute. Revenue visibility comes from how full their order book is and how fast new orders flow in each quarter. Execution is the risky part — one badly run project can hurt margins for a year.`,examples:["CUMMINSIND","SCHAEFFLER","SKFINDIA","TIMKEN","TIINDIA","RATNAMANI","WELCORP","TITAGARH","APLAPOLLO"]},{name:"Cables & Wires",oneLiner:"Specialised subset of Electrical — the actual cables, conductors, and wires made at scale.",details:"These are the companies that make the wires running inside your walls and the thick power cables carrying electricity from substations to cities. Their fortunes swing with copper and aluminium prices (their main raw material), housing demand, transmission line buildout, and exports. They tend to benefit earliest in a real-estate and power capex upcycle — wiring is one of the first things ordered when a new building or substation starts.",examples:["POLYCAB","KEI","FINCABLES","RRKABEL","STLTECH","UNIVCABLES"]},{name:"Castings & Forgings",oneLiner:"Raw metal parts — forged and cast components for autos, wind, defence, and oil & gas.",details:"These are the companies that take lumps of hot steel and shape them — by hammering (forging) or pouring into moulds (casting) — into engine parts, crankshafts, wind-turbine hubs, artillery components, oil-rig fittings. They are upstream suppliers to everyone who builds heavy machinery. Because a lot of their sales go abroad (auto OEMs in US/Europe, wind in Europe, aerospace globally), the rupee and global factory activity matter a lot to their numbers.",examples:["BHARATFORG","AIAENG","RKFORGE","HAPPYFORGE","ROLEXRINGS","KALYANIFRG","MMFL"]},{name:"Industrial Products",oneLiner:"A catch-all for speciality industrial goods — bearings, compressors, pumps, valves, abrasives.",details:"The small but mission-critical components that make bigger machines work. A bearing in a car, a pump in a refinery, a valve in a pipeline, a compressor in an air-conditioner factory. Demand is steady in normal times but accelerates sharply when factories and plants are being built. This segment often leads the rest of Industrials — when bearing and pump orders rise, bigger engineering orders usually follow a quarter or two later.",examples:["GRINDWELL","KSB","KIRLOSBROS","INGERRAND","CARBORUNIV","ELGIEQUIP","HEG","GRAPHITE","SHAKTIPUMP"]},{name:"Packaging",oneLiner:"Packaging materials and solutions — containers, films, bottles, labels, tubes.",details:"The plastic bottles, flexible films, paperboard boxes, aluminium tubes, and labels that wrap the products you buy. Everything from shampoo bottles to pharma blister packs to food pouches to e-commerce cartons. Demand is driven by FMCG volume growth, pharma packaging, e-commerce shipping, and raw-material costs (plastic granules, paper, metal). It is less cyclical than the rest of Industrials — more a steady-compounding segment with occasional bursts when FMCG growth accelerates or input costs cool.",examples:["UFLEX","POLYPLEX","EPL","TCPLPACK","MOLDTKPAC","HUHTAMAKI","COSMOFIRST"]}],siblingsTitle:"Momentum siblings",siblingsParas:["Industrials rarely moves one industry at a time. When a real-estate upcycle begins, Cables & Wires tends to move first — they are the earliest product to get ordered on a new construction site. A few weeks later Electrical Equipment picks up as switchgear, meters, and wiring systems get spec'd in. A quarter or two later, the larger Engineering and EPC names start reflecting the wave in their order books.","Similarly, Industrial Products often leads Engineering — bearings, valves, pumps, and compressors get ordered before the big turnkey projects actually roll. Castings & Forgings tend to move with global auto and wind-energy cycles, sometimes independently of domestic Industrials, but they anchor the manufacturing end of the sector."],driversTitle:"What moves this sector",drivers:["Government capex outlay — especially in power, railways, defence, and infrastructure","Private-sector capex revival — corporate expansion, new factories, capacity additions","Real-estate construction cycle — drives cables, wires, switchgear, electrical goods","Power transmission & distribution upgrades — transformers, smart meters, grid modernisation","Renewable energy buildout — solar parks, wind farms, hydrogen, storage","Global manufacturing PMI — export-heavy names like forgings and bearings respond to this","Raw-material prices — copper, aluminium, steel swings affect margins across the sector","Interest rate direction — lower rates accelerate project approvals and capex"],closingQuote:"Industrials is the quiet engine behind almost every market story — when it accelerates broadly, the economy is usually not far behind."},o={kicker:"KNOW YOUR SECTOR",headline:"Aerospace & Defence",tagline:"Weapons, warships, aircraft, and the systems that run them",overview:["Aerospace & Defence is the set of companies that design and manufacture military hardware and aerospace systems — fighter aircraft, helicopters, missiles, artillery, warships, radars, electronics, and the components that go inside them. The customer base is narrow and lumpy: mainly the Indian armed forces, some export clients, and a growing slice of private-sector aviation and defence exports.","The sector has transformed in the last decade on the back of the Make-in-India push, indigenisation lists that force domestic sourcing, and rising defence exports. Orders are large, multi-year, and awarded by the Ministry of Defence — which means revenue visibility once an order is booked is unusually high, but order wins are lumpy and political. Momentum here usually reacts to budget announcements, large contract awards, and geopolitical tension.",e(1)],industries:[{name:"Defence",oneLiner:"Military hardware, aerospace systems, and the electronics that run them.",details:"These companies make the weapons and platforms of the Indian armed forces — fighter jets and helicopters, missiles and torpedoes, warships, artillery guns, armoured vehicles, radars, communication systems, and all the sub-components that go inside them. They win lumpy, multi-year contracts from the Ministry of Defence; a few also export to friendly countries. The order book is the single most important number — when an order worth a few thousand crore lands, revenue gets visibility for the next 3-5 years. Large listed players include the shipbuilders, aircraft makers, electronics houses, and private component and drone makers.",examples:["HAL","BEL","BDL","MAZDOCK","COCHINSHIP","GRSE","SOLARINDS","DATAPATTNS","ASTRAMICRO","MTARTECH","PARAS","ZENTEC","DCXINDIA","IDEAFORGE"]}],siblingsTitle:"Momentum siblings",siblingsParas:["Because Defence is the only industry in this sector, classic sector-sibling rotation does not apply the same way it does elsewhere. Instead, siblings appear within the industry itself — shipbuilders tend to move as a pack when naval orders flow, aircraft/helicopter names move together on air-force orders, electronics and component makers move as a pack when indigenisation contracts land.","Also worth watching is the overlap with Industrials — especially Engineering and Castings & Forgings — because many defence contracts pull through heavy engineering and forged-parts orders upstream. A strong Defence cycle often brings Industrial sub-industries along for the ride."],driversTitle:"What moves this sector",drivers:["Central defence budget — capital outlay and acquisition allocation","Major contract awards — aircraft, helicopter, warship, missile, radar orders","Indigenisation lists and Make-in-India policy — forces domestic sourcing","Defence export approvals and deals — opens a new revenue channel","Geopolitical tension — tends to pull forward procurement urgency","Order book build-up and execution pace — visible in quarterly results","Private-sector entry — drones, electronics, component outsourcing contracts","Raw-material and electronics input costs — steel, titanium, semiconductors"],closingQuote:"Defence is the slowest-moving but most sticky sector — once an order is booked, the revenue is almost guaranteed, but the stock moves when the order lands."},l={kicker:"KNOW YOUR SECTOR",headline:"Automobile",tagline:"The cars, two-wheelers, trucks, tyres, and parts that move the country",overview:["Automobile is one of India's largest industrial sectors — it covers the companies that make passenger cars, two-wheelers, tractors, commercial vehicles, the ancillary parts that go inside them, and the tyres that roll on the road. It is a massive manufacturing ecosystem spread across Chennai, Pune, Gurgaon, Sanand, and countless smaller hubs.","The sector is highly cyclical and touches almost every macro theme — rural income, urban consumption, fuel prices, interest rates, commodity prices, and now the EV transition. A single good monsoon lifts tractor demand; a rate cut lifts car and two-wheeler financing; a commodity crash lifts margins across the board. In stock-market terms, Automobile is where macro narratives first show up as hard numbers.",e(3)],industries:[{name:"Automobiles",oneLiner:"Original equipment makers — the brands behind cars, two-wheelers, tractors, and trucks.",details:"These are the companies whose badge you see on the vehicle — passenger car makers, two-wheeler houses, tractor companies, commercial-vehicle and bus manufacturers. They assemble the final product, set the price, and carry the brand. Revenue swings with monthly sales volumes (reported on the first of every month), model launches, festive-season demand, and the mix between entry-level and premium. Margins are sensitive to steel, aluminium, rubber, and semiconductor costs.",examples:["MARUTI","M&M","TVSMOTOR","BAJAJ-AUTO","HEROMOTOCO","EICHERMOT","ASHOKLEY","ESCORTS","HYUNDAI","OLECTRA","FORCEMOT","OLAELEC"]},{name:"Auto Ancillaries",oneLiner:"The parts ecosystem — everything inside a vehicle that the badge-maker doesn't make itself.",details:"These are the companies that supply components to the automakers — engines, transmissions, axles, batteries, lighting, interiors, brakes, wiring harnesses, casting parts, plastics, electronics. A modern car has 20,000+ parts; only a fraction are made in-house. Ancillaries follow OEM volumes closely but also benefit from content-per-vehicle going up — as cars get more electronic, more safety-equipped, and eventually electric, the rupee value of parts per vehicle rises. Many also export to global OEMs.",examples:["BOSCHLTD","SUNDRMFAST","UNOMINDA","ENDURANCE","LUMAXIND","GABRIEL","SUBROS","ARE&M","CRAFTSMAN","SANSERA","FIEMIND","INDNIPPON"]},{name:"Tyres",oneLiner:"Rubber on the road — replacement tyres, OE tyres, and truck/bus radials.",details:"Tyre companies make the rubber that every vehicle runs on. Demand comes from two streams — original equipment (tyres fitted on new vehicles at the factory) and replacement (every 40,000-60,000 km). Replacement is the bigger, steadier, higher-margin business. Rubber price, crude-oil-linked synthetic rubber, and carbon black dominate the cost side; protectionist tariffs on Chinese tyres have been a tailwind. Trucks and two-wheelers are the key volume drivers.",examples:["MRF","APOLLOTYRE","CEATLTD","JKTYRE","BALKRISIND","TVSSRICHAK"]}],siblingsTitle:"Momentum siblings",siblingsParas:["The Automobile sector moves in a predictable sequence. Automobiles themselves — the OEMs — are the headline: monthly sales numbers drive narrative. A few weeks later, Auto Ancillaries catch up — they supply to those OEMs, so a volume rebound at Maruti or Tata Motors flows to the component makers within a quarter. Tyres are slightly lagged because replacement demand is somewhat independent of new-vehicle sales, but a strong OEM cycle still pulls them along over 2-3 months.","In a downcycle the order reverses — Ancillaries weaken first as OEMs cut production, then OEMs report soft monthly numbers, and Tyres hold up last because replacement demand is stickier."],driversTitle:"What moves this sector",drivers:["Monthly sales volumes — reported first of every month, the single biggest catalyst","Interest rates — directly affects car, two-wheeler, and commercial-vehicle financing","Rural income and monsoon — drives tractor and entry-level two-wheeler demand","Fuel prices — petrol/diesel costs, and the relative economics of CNG and EV","Commodity prices — steel, aluminium, rubber, copper swing margins","Semiconductor availability — supply shocks can cap production","EV policy, subsidies, and charging infrastructure — reshapes the mix","Festive season demand (Sep-Nov) — the biggest retail window of the year"],closingQuote:"Automobile is where every macro theme — rates, commodities, monsoons, EV policy — eventually shows up in monthly sales data."},c={kicker:"KNOW YOUR SECTOR",headline:"Chemicals",tagline:"The invisible raw materials of every manufactured product",overview:["Chemicals is the sector that supplies the building blocks for almost every manufactured product. Paints, textiles, pharmaceuticals, plastics, fertilisers, detergents, adhesives — none of them exist without the chemical industry upstream. Indian chemicals has three big tailwinds over the last decade: the China+1 export push, strong specialty chemistry R&D, and deep agrochemical demand from Indian and global agriculture.","The sector splits cleanly into commodity (low-margin, volume-driven, price-sensitive) and specialty (higher-margin, R&D-led, customer-stickier). Commodity chemical prices move with crude oil, natural gas, and global supply shocks; specialty chemical momentum is tied to export orders, multi-year contracts with global MNCs, and product launches. Agrochemicals and fertilisers have their own rhythm — they swing with Indian monsoons, global soft-commodity prices, and government subsidy policy.",e(4)],industries:[{name:"Specialty Chemicals",oneLiner:"High-margin, low-volume chemicals engineered for a specific end-use.",details:"These are the companies that make custom chemicals for a specific customer and a specific application — fluorochemicals, advanced intermediates, custom synthesis for global pharma and agrochem MNCs, performance additives. The customer is often a global multinational locked into a long contract; competition is limited by R&D know-how and process chemistry. India has emerged as a specialty chemicals hub on the back of the China+1 shift — global companies want a supply source outside China, and Indian players with clean compliance records have benefitted enormously.",examples:["SRF","NAVINFLUOR","ATUL","FINEORG","DEEPAKNTR","VINATIORGA","PIDILITIND","ALKYLAMINE","NEOGEN","JUBLCPL","BASF","LINDEINDIA","GALAXYSURF","AETHER"]},{name:"Basic Chemicals",oneLiner:"Commodity building-block chemicals — caustic soda, chlorine, soda ash, acids.",details:"The workhorse chemicals that feed every downstream industry. Caustic soda goes into aluminium, textiles, paper, soap. Soda ash goes into glass and detergents. Chlorine, sulphuric acid, hydrogen peroxide all have huge downstream customer bases. These are volume businesses — margins depend heavily on global prices, power costs (caustic soda is very power-intensive), and domestic demand cycles. When global chemical prices firm up, basic chemicals report cleanly in their margins.",examples:["TATACHEM","GHCL","GUJALKALI","GNFC","ANDHRSUGAR"]},{name:"Agrochemicals",oneLiner:"Crop-protection chemicals — insecticides, herbicides, fungicides, seed treatments.",details:"Companies that protect the farmer's crop from pests, weeds, and diseases. The Indian agrochemical industry has grown into a global export powerhouse — many names supply generic and proprietary molecules to farmers worldwide and to global majors like Syngenta and BASF. Domestic demand swings with monsoons and crop mix; export demand swings with global soft commodities (when soybean or corn prices are high, farmers worldwide spend more on crop protection).",examples:["UPL","PIIND","BAYERCROP","RALLIS","DHANUKA","SUMICHEM","INSECTICID","SHARDACROP","HERANBA","NACLIND","BHARATRAS"]},{name:"Fertilizers",oneLiner:"Urea, DAP, complex fertilisers — the nutrition the farm soil needs.",details:"These companies make and distribute the nitrogen, phosphate, and potash-based fertilisers that Indian agriculture runs on. The business is deeply tied to government subsidy policy — the government controls retail prices and reimburses the companies via subsidy. Global gas prices (urea uses natural gas), global phosphate/potash prices, and monsoon quality are the three swing factors. Nutrient-based subsidy policy changes can create big earnings swings in a single quarter.",examples:["COROMANDEL","CHAMBLFERT","GSFC","RCF","NFL","FACT","PARADEEP","ZUARI"]}],siblingsTitle:"Momentum siblings",siblingsParas:["Specialty Chemicals and Basic Chemicals often move together during a global chemical price upcycle — global shortages firm up commodity prices first, and a few quarters later speciality order inflows improve as customers de-stock and re-stock. Agrochemicals and Fertilizers track Indian monsoon forecasts and global soft-commodity prices together — a good monsoon plus firm soybean prices is the cleanest bull setup for both.","Specialty Chemicals also has a partial overlap with Healthcare (Pharma) — many specialty chemical companies supply custom intermediates to pharma and vice versa, so a strong global pharma spend cycle occasionally shows up in both."],driversTitle:"What moves this sector",drivers:["China+1 export shift — structural tailwind for Indian specialty chemicals","Global chemical prices — MDI, TDI, caustic soda, urea benchmark prices","Crude oil and natural gas — inputs to almost every chemical","Monsoon and Indian agriculture cycle — critical for agrochemicals and fertilisers","Government subsidy policy — nutrient-based subsidy, urea pricing","Global soft-commodity prices — soybean, corn, wheat lift agrochemical demand","US and EU customer capex plans — drives specialty chemical order inflows","Environmental compliance tightening — favours cleaner Indian producers"],closingQuote:"Chemicals is the single biggest beneficiary of the China+1 industrial shift — patient investors in the right specialty names have compounded for a decade."},d={kicker:"KNOW YOUR SECTOR",headline:"Construction & Real Estate",tagline:"Land, buildings, highways, and the firms that build them",overview:["Construction & Real Estate covers everything from residential and commercial property developers to the EPC contractors building highways, metros, airports, bridges, and pipelines. It is one of the most interest-rate-sensitive sectors in the market and one of the biggest employers in the country.","The sector has two distinct rhythms. Real-estate developers move with home-buyer demand — mortgage rates, affordability, urbanisation, and income growth. Infrastructure and EPC players move with government capex — budget allocation, project awards by NHAI and Railway, and execution pace. Both can rally together in a strong economic cycle but often diverge — private residential demand and public infra capex are driven by different buttons.",e(3)],industries:[{name:"Real Estate",oneLiner:"Residential, commercial, and industrial property developers.",details:"These companies buy land, build apartments and offices, and sell or lease them. The listed universe is dominated by a handful of large developers, mostly concentrated in Mumbai, Bengaluru, NCR, Pune, and Hyderabad. Revenue is recognised on project completion, so reported numbers lag actual pre-sales by a year or two — pre-sales is the leading indicator to watch. Debt levels, land bank quality, and launch pipelines are the three health checks. Commercial real-estate (offices, malls) moves differently — it tracks corporate expansion and REIT flows.",examples:["DLF","GODREJPROP","OBEROIRLTY","PRESTIGE","BRIGADE","SOBHA","SUNTECK","ANANTRAJ","MAHLIFE","KOLTEPATIL","SIGNATURE","ABREL"]},{name:"Infrastructure & Construction",oneLiner:"Highways, bridges, metros, airports, pipelines — the civil contractors behind public infra.",details:"These are the companies that actually pour concrete on the National Highway, lay railway track, dig metro tunnels, and build airports. They win government tenders, execute over 2-4 years, and report revenue based on percentage completion. Order book size and order book-to-revenue ratio are the two numbers to track — a healthy 3x order-book cover gives revenue visibility for the next three years. Execution is capital-intensive and margin-sensitive; delayed payments from government clients can hurt working capital.",examples:["LODHA","PHOENIXLTD","HGINFRA","GRINFRA","NESCO","AHLUCONT","DBL","JKIL","PSPPROJECT","KALPATARU","AFCONS","ASHIANA"]},{name:"EPC & Engineering Services",oneLiner:"Engineering-procurement-construction firms and specialised project service providers.",details:"Close cousins to Infrastructure & Construction, but more engineering-led — they handle the design and specialised execution of complex projects: power plants, transmission lines, water treatment plants, industrial complexes, oil & gas infrastructure. The line with Industrials (Engineering) is blurry — many large players operate across both. Revenue and order-book dynamics are similar to Infrastructure & Construction; margin profiles tend to be slightly better due to the engineering content.",examples:["LT","KEC","KPIL","ENGINERSIN","NCC","IRCON","ASHOKA","WABAG","ISGEC","POWERMECH","TRANSRAILL","TECHNOE"]}],siblingsTitle:"Momentum siblings",siblingsParas:["Infrastructure & Construction and EPC & Engineering Services move almost in lockstep — both are order-book driven, both depend on government capex, both are exposed to the same pool of raw materials and labour. When the central Budget allocates heavily to infra, both industries tend to rally. Real Estate runs on a different beat — rate cuts and housing affordability move it, often independently of infra.","Real Estate also has cross-sector siblings worth watching: a strong residential cycle pulls Cables & Wires, Electrical Equipment, Cement, Building Materials, Paints and Tiles along with it. A home-buyer boom lifts every product that goes inside the house — if you see a big Real Estate rally, the Industrials and Materials sectors often have winners appearing at the same time."],driversTitle:"What moves this sector",drivers:["Interest rates — single biggest swing factor for real-estate demand","Central budget infrastructure allocation — especially roads, railways, urban transport","Project awards by NHAI, Railways, and state agencies — lumpy but high-impact","Urban land prices and regulatory approvals — affect developer margins","REIT flows and commercial office demand — drive commercial real estate","Commodity prices — cement, steel, copper affect both developers and contractors","Working capital and payment cycles — government payment delays can hurt contractors","Housing subsidy and affordable-housing schemes — lift entry-level demand"],closingQuote:"Construction & Real Estate sits at the intersection of interest rates and government capex — when both turn friendly at the same time, this is where the biggest moves happen."},m={kicker:"KNOW YOUR SECTOR",headline:"Consumer Discretionary",tagline:"Things people buy when they have spare income",overview:["Consumer Discretionary is where Indians spend their disposable income — on a new television, a diamond ring, a holiday, a pair of sneakers, a consumer-brand purchase. Unlike consumer staples (which people buy every week regardless), discretionary spending goes up when income rises and down when it doesn't. That makes this sector a clean read on middle-class and premium-segment income health.","The sector has benefited massively from premiumisation, urban aspirational spending, and the growth of organised retail. But it is also the first to get hit in a slowdown — consumers cut holidays and big-ticket purchases before they cut groceries. Watch urban unemployment, credit-card spend data, and festive-season numbers for clean signals.",e(7)],industries:[{name:"Consumer Electronics",oneLiner:"Branded white goods — TVs, ACs, washing machines, refrigerators, kitchen appliances.",details:"These are the companies that put the branded white-goods in your home. Air-conditioners (growing 20%+ in good summers), TVs, washing machines, refrigerators, kitchen appliances, fans, water purifiers. Demand is highly seasonal — ACs peak in summer, heaters in winter, everything peaks around Diwali. Margins are under pressure from Chinese imports and aggressive pricing, but premiumisation (smart TVs, inverter ACs) has lifted average selling prices over the decade.",examples:["VOLTAS","WHIRLPOOL","BLUESTARCO","SYMPHONY","BAJAJELEC","TTKPRESTIG","ORIENTELEC","DIXON","STOVEKRAFT","EUREKAFORB","IFBIND","LGEINDIA"]},{name:"Consumer Products",oneLiner:"Branded lifestyle goods — innerwear, eyewear, watches, kitchen brands, durables.",details:"A catch-all for branded consumer goods that aren't electronics — innerwear and apparel brands, eyewear chains, watch brands, branded kitchenware, pens and stationery brands, home furnishings. Many of these categories are rapidly shifting from unorganised to organised — growth isn't just market growth, it's also share-gain from informal players. Brand strength, distribution depth, and product innovation are the three moats worth tracking.",examples:["PAGEIND","HAVELLS","MANYAVAR","SHOPERSTOP","KPRMILL","KKCL","DOLLAR","KITEX","CARTRADE","CRIZAC","V2RETAIL","STANLEY","CROMPTON"]},{name:"Electronics",oneLiner:"Electronic manufacturing services (EMS) — the makers behind the brands.",details:"These companies don't own the brands — they manufacture electronic products for other companies (mobile phones, appliances, automotive electronics, LED lighting, hearables). India has emerged as a global EMS destination thanks to PLI schemes and the China+1 manufacturing shift. Margins are thin (3-7%) but volume growth has been spectacular — revenue compounding at 25-40% for leaders. Every new mobile phone launch, every new appliance line, every import-substitution push helps.",examples:["AMBER","KAYNES","SYRMA","CYIENTDLM","AVALON","HONAUT","CENTUM","ELIN","KERNEX","PGEL"]},{name:"Gems & Jewellery",oneLiner:"Gold, diamond, and branded jewellery retailers.",details:"These are the companies behind the jewellery showrooms you see in every Indian city. Revenue swings with gold prices (when prices fall, buyers rush in; when they rise fast, buying pauses), with wedding season, and with festival demand (Akshaya Tritiya, Dhanteras). Organised jewellery retail is rapidly taking share from small family shops — trust, hallmarking, and financing offers are the key weapons. Studded (diamond) jewellery carries better margins than plain gold.",examples:["TITAN","KALYANKJIL","SENCO","PNGJL","PCJEWELLER","RAJESHEXPO","THANGAMAYL","TBZ","GOLDIAM","MOTISONS","SKYGOLD"]},{name:"Hotels & Tourism",oneLiner:"Hotel chains, resort operators, and large hospitality brands.",details:"Hotel companies run properties (owned, leased, or managed). Revenue is measured in RevPAR (revenue per available room) — a combination of occupancy and average room rate. Post-COVID, Indian hospitality has had a spectacular rebound — corporate travel, weddings, and domestic leisure have pushed RevPAR to all-time highs. The sector is asset-heavy; operating leverage is huge, so small demand swings create big margin swings.",examples:["INDHOTEL","EIHOTEL","CHALET","TAJGVK","ITCHOTELS","JUBLFOOD","MHRIL","LEMONTREE","WESTLIFE","SAMHI","JUNIPER","TRAVELFOOD"]},{name:"Leather & Footwear",oneLiner:"Branded footwear, leather goods, and school-shoe manufacturers.",details:"Footwear in India is split between organised (Bata, Relaxo, Metro, Liberty, Campus) and unorganised (local shops). Demand drivers are similar to consumer products — brand premiumisation, store-expansion, and festive demand. Margins depend on rubber and leather prices. School shoes carry a seasonal spike around June and December.",examples:["BATAINDIA","RELAXO","METROBRAND","CAMPUS","LIBERTSHOE","KHADIM","MIRZAINT","SUPERHOUSE"]},{name:"Trading & Distribution",oneLiner:"Wholesale distributors, retail chains, and multi-brand trading houses.",details:"These companies sit between brands and consumers — supermarket chains, multi-brand fashion retail, electronics-distribution houses, general-merchandise retailers. Revenue depends on same-store-sales growth (SSSG), store-count expansion, and gross-margin on private labels. E-commerce is both a competitor and a distribution channel. Inventory turns and working capital are the key operating metrics.",examples:["DMART","TRENT","ADANIENT","VMART","HONASA","ETHOSLTD","MEDPLUS","CELLO","DLINKINDIA","LANDMARK","ARVINDFASN"]}],siblingsTitle:"Momentum siblings",siblingsParas:["Consumer Discretionary tends to rally as a pack when consumer sentiment turns — Consumer Electronics (especially ACs in a hot summer), Jewellery (when wedding season is good), Hotels & Tourism (when corporate travel revives), and Consumer Products typically move within the same 2-3 month window. Electronics (the EMS names) has its own beat — it moves more with PLI-scheme awards, new product launches for global customers, and capacity ramp-up milestones.","In a slowdown, the Trading & Distribution names (especially discretionary retail) and Jewellery tend to show strain first as big-ticket purchases get deferred. Consumer Electronics lasts longer because of replacement cycles. Hotels are very cyclical — they boom in good times and crash fast in bad ones."],driversTitle:"What moves this sector",drivers:["Urban disposable income and consumer sentiment — the single biggest swing factor","Festive season (Sep-Nov) — the biggest retail window of the year","Wedding season — big for jewellery, apparel, footwear, hotels","Gold prices — inverse effect on jewellery demand (cheaper gold = more buying)","Summer intensity — drives AC, fan, cooler demand","Corporate travel revival — critical for hotels, airlines, premium retail","PLI schemes and manufacturing policy — structural for Electronics/EMS","Premiumisation and brand-led share shifts from unorganised to organised"],closingQuote:"Consumer Discretionary is the clearest real-time read on middle-class India's income health — every rate cut, every good monsoon, every festive season shows up here first."},h={kicker:"KNOW YOUR SECTOR",headline:"Consumer Staples",tagline:"The everyday necessities that every household buys regardless of the cycle",overview:["Consumer Staples is what Indian households buy every week — packaged food, soap, shampoo, toothpaste, tea, biscuits, edible oil, cigarettes, beverages. Demand is inelastic: people don't stop brushing teeth or eating rice because the economy is weak. That makes this the most defensive sector in the market — slow-and-steady earnings growth, low cyclicality, high return-on-capital, consistent dividends.","The sector lives on two levers: volume growth (how many units they sell, driven by rural and urban demand) and price/mix (realisation per unit, driven by premiumisation and price hikes). Raw material costs (palm oil, crude-oil-linked packaging, wheat, milk, cocoa) are the biggest margin swing factor. FMCG is also uniquely exposed to rural India — rural is half the volume, so monsoon quality and rural wage data matter a lot.",e(6)],industries:[{name:"FMCG - Diversified",oneLiner:"Large multi-category FMCG houses — soaps, detergents, foods, personal care, home care.",details:"These are the umbrella FMCG companies that sell across many categories — a single company might sell soap, shampoo, tea, noodles, biscuits, detergent, spreads, and dairy. Revenue is a mix of volume growth (4-8% in normal times) and price-led growth (3-5%). Rural and urban are roughly 50:50 by revenue; rural slows the FMCG engine when monsoons fail. Margins depend on palm oil, crude oil (packaging), and milk prices.",examples:["MARICO","PATANJALI","KSCL","AWL","GAEL","VENKEYS","GOKULAGRO","HNDFDS","TINNARUBR","MODINATUR","NATHBIOGEN"]},{name:"Food Processing",oneLiner:"Packaged and branded food — biscuits, snacks, dairy, ready-to-eat, edible oils.",details:"Companies focused specifically on branded food — packaged snacks, dairy, bakery, confectionery, frozen foods, and edible oils. Demand drivers are modern-retail penetration, convenience-driven urban consumption, and premiumisation. Margin sensitivity to agricultural inputs (wheat, milk, sugar, palm oil) is very high — a spike in palm oil can compress margins for two quarters at a stretch.",examples:["BRITANNIA","NESTLEIND","HATSUN","BIKAJI","HERITGFOOD","VBL","KRBL","LTFOODS","ZYDUSWELL","GODREJAGRO","DODLA","AVANTIFEED","ORKLAINDIA","VADILALIND","TASTYBITE"]},{name:"Personal Care",oneLiner:"Soaps, shampoos, skincare, cosmetics, oral care — the daily hygiene basket.",details:"Companies focused on personal hygiene and beauty — bar soaps, liquid body wash, shampoos, conditioners, toothpaste, skincare, deodorants, colour cosmetics. These categories have some of the highest gross margins in FMCG — brand pricing power is strong. Premiumisation (moving from basic to naturals, to premium, to luxury) is the structural growth lever. Rural penetration for premium personal care is still low, so long runway remains.",examples:["HINDUNILVR","COLPAL","DABUR","GODREJCP","EMAMILTD","GILLETTE","JYOTHYLAB","BAJAJCON","PGHH"]},{name:"Beverages & Spirits",oneLiner:"Branded alcoholic drinks, beers, and non-alcoholic beverages.",details:"The listed beverages universe in India is dominated by alcoholic drink makers — whisky, rum, beer. Demand is stable-to-growing thanks to demographics and urbanisation, but state-government excise policy is the big swing factor — a state-level excise reset can move revenue materially in a single quarter. Premiumisation (people moving from regular to premium whisky or craft beer) is the structural theme. Non-alcoholic beverages are mostly private (Pepsi, Coke, Dabur's segment) with a few listed beneficiaries.",examples:["UNITDSPR","UBL","RADICO","TATACONSUM","GMBREW","SULA","CCL","ABDL","INDIAGLYCO","GLOBUSSPR"]},{name:"Tobacco",oneLiner:"Cigarettes and tobacco products — largely a single-company show.",details:"Listed tobacco in India is essentially ITC's cigarette business plus a few smaller players. Cigarette volumes swing with excise duty hikes (higher excise = volume down, revenue often still up because of price pass-through). It is extremely high-margin, high-cash-generating, and defensive. The regulatory overhang (excise, health warnings, illegal-cigarette competition) is permanent; investors price it in.",examples:["ITC","VSTIND","GODFRYPHLP"]},{name:"Sugar & Ethanol",oneLiner:"Sugar producers and the ethanol blending opportunity tied to it.",details:"Sugar companies crush cane into sugar, and increasingly divert cane juice to ethanol for fuel blending. The economics depend on Indian sugar cycle (global prices, domestic stocks, MSP), monsoon quality in sugar-growing states (UP, Maharashtra, Karnataka), and the government's ethanol-blending target (currently E20 by 2025). Ethanol has been the structural positive — it monetises cane at better margins and reduces the over-supply problem that used to hit sugar stocks.",examples:["BALRAMCHIN","TRIVENI","DALMIASUG","DHAMPURSUG","BAJAJHIND","EIDPARRY","UGARSUGAR","RENUKA","GODAVARIB","DCMSRIND","ZUARIIND"]}],siblingsTitle:"Momentum siblings",siblingsParas:["Consumer Staples moves as a pack on three triggers: rural demand revival, commodity-cost cooldown, and festive demand. When palm oil or wheat prices fall, FMCG - Diversified, Food Processing, and Personal Care all catch a margin tailwind together. When rural wages improve, the mass-market heavy names (especially Marico, Dabur, Godrej Consumer) outperform first.","Sugar & Ethanol moves on its own rhythm — it tracks sugar cycle, monsoon quality, and ethanol-blending policy announcements. Tobacco mostly moves on excise news and cigarette-volume data rather than the broader FMCG cycle."],driversTitle:"What moves this sector",drivers:["Rural income and monsoon — half of FMCG volume comes from rural India","Raw-material costs — palm oil, crude oil, wheat, milk, cocoa swing margins","Urban consumption sentiment and premiumisation trends","Festive season and wedding season demand peaks","GST and excise policy — especially for Tobacco and Beverages & Spirits","Ethanol-blending targets — structural for Sugar & Ethanol","E-commerce and quick-commerce penetration — reshaping distribution","Currency and palm-oil imports — FMCG is a net importer of agri-commodities"],closingQuote:"Consumer Staples is the slow-compounding backbone of Indian equities — steady earnings growth, high return ratios, and the sector that delivers even when nothing else does."},u={kicker:"KNOW YOUR SECTOR",headline:"Energy",tagline:"Oil, gas, electricity — the fuel that powers the economy",overview:["Energy is the sector that supplies the country's fuel and power — upstream oil and gas exploration, downstream refining and retail, natural gas distribution, electricity generation (thermal, hydro, nuclear, solar, wind), and power transmission. It is a capital-intensive, heavily regulated sector dominated by state-owned giants, but private players have a growing role especially in renewables and gas distribution.","Oil & Gas earnings swing with global crude prices and refining margins. Power earnings are driven by capacity utilisation, power-purchase-agreement (PPA) rates, and increasingly the renewables transition. The sector has been reshaped in the last five years by the energy transition — solar and wind capacity has exploded, green hydrogen is on the horizon, and thermal power is slowly becoming a peaking fuel rather than baseload.",e(2)],industries:[{name:"Oil & Gas",oneLiner:"Upstream explorers, refiners, gas distributors, and integrated oil majors.",details:'This industry covers the full oil value chain — upstream exploration and production (E&P), downstream refining, petrol and diesel retail, natural gas transmission and distribution, and petrochemicals. Integrated majors combine several of these. Refining margins (the "crack spread" between crude cost and fuel selling price) are the single biggest swing factor for downstream. Upstream players live on crude prices. Gas distribution (CGD) is a more stable regulated-return business.',examples:["RELIANCE","ONGC","IOC","BPCL","HINDPETRO","GAIL","PETRONET","IGL","MGL","GSPL","OIL","GUJGASLTD","ATGL","MRPL"]},{name:"Power & Utilities",oneLiner:"Electricity generators, renewable energy developers, and transmission companies.",details:"Companies that generate and transmit electricity. Mix includes thermal (coal), hydro, nuclear, solar, and wind generation, plus high-voltage transmission and state-utility distribution. Returns are largely regulated (tariff orders fix return on equity), but renewables and private thermal assets can earn better returns in tight markets. India's power-demand growth (6-8% annually) combined with the push to renewable capacity has made this sector one of the clearest structural growth stories.",examples:["NTPC","POWERGRID","TATAPOWER","ADANIPOWER","ADANIGREEN","JSWENERGY","TORNTPOWER","CESC","NLCINDIA","KPIGREEN","ADANIENSOL"]}],siblingsTitle:"Momentum siblings",siblingsParas:["Oil & Gas and Power & Utilities are two separate beats inside Energy. Oil & Gas moves on global crude prices, refining-margin cycles, and GRM (gross refining margin) outlook. A crude-price crash often triggers a refining-margin boom (because fuel retail prices don't fall as fast) — downstream refiners catch a sharp, short-lived rally while upstream E&P names weaken. Power moves differently — a hot summer spikes electricity demand and the thermal and renewable generators rally; a capex announcement in transmission lifts PowerGrid and the transmission-linked names.","Notice also that Power & Utilities has a strong sibling effect with Industrials — Transformers, Cables & Wires, Electrical Equipment, and Engineering all benefit when power capex accelerates. A Power sector rally often drags these up in parallel."],driversTitle:"What moves this sector",drivers:["Global crude oil prices — single biggest variable for oil & gas","Refining margins (GRM) — the spread between crude cost and fuel selling price","Power demand growth — summer peaks, industrial recovery, EV charging","Renewable energy tenders and PLI schemes — structural for solar/wind","Regulatory tariff orders — fix returns for transmission and regulated thermal","Natural gas prices — affect CGD, petrochemicals, fertilisers downstream","Grid modernisation and transmission capex — lifts PowerGrid and equipment makers","Energy transition policy — green hydrogen, storage, carbon targets"],closingQuote:"Energy is where the old-economy cash flow meets the new-economy transition — the next decade will separate the well-managed integrated names from the rest."},p={kicker:"KNOW YOUR SECTOR",headline:"Financial Services",tagline:"Banks, NBFCs, insurers, and the plumbing of Indian capital markets",overview:["Financial Services is the biggest single sector in the Nifty by weight. It covers the banks (both private and public-sector), non-bank lenders (NBFCs), housing finance companies, capital-market intermediaries (broking, asset management, exchanges, depositories), and insurance companies. Whatever else the economy does, it runs on credit — and Financial Services is where credit flows.","The sector is highly sensitive to the RBI rate cycle and to asset-quality cycles. Banks and lenders benefit from rate hikes short-term (deposit re-pricing lags lending rates) but suffer when rates go too high and borrowers default. Capital-market names (brokers, AMCs) move with market sentiment and trading volumes. Insurance moves with premium growth and investment-book returns.",e(6)],industries:[{name:"Banks - Private",oneLiner:"Private-sector banks — the growth and efficiency end of Indian banking.",details:"These are the privately-owned banks, generally with higher return-on-equity, lower bad loans, stronger technology, and better cost ratios than the state-owned counterparts. The big-four (HDFC, ICICI, Axis, Kotak) dominate but mid-tier private banks (IndusInd, IDFC First, Federal) have their own stories. Key metrics: net interest margin (NIM), loan growth, credit cost (provisioning), and CASA ratio (share of low-cost deposits).",examples:["HDFCBANK","ICICIBANK","AXISBANK","KOTAKBANK","INDUSINDBK","IDFCFIRSTB","FEDERALBNK","BANDHANBNK","RBLBANK","CUB","KARURVYSYA","AUBANK","KTKBANK","DCBBANK","J&KBANK"]},{name:"Banks - PSU",oneLiner:"Public-sector banks — government-owned, large deposit base, deep rural reach.",details:"State-owned banks dominated by SBI and a handful of PSU peers. Traditionally they carried more bad loans and lower return-on-equity than privates, but the last 5 years of recapitalisation, digital upgrades, and aggressive credit growth have brought a re-rating. They have the widest rural footprint, the largest deposit bases, and benefit most from government-linked banking (payments, subsidy transfer, rural credit).",examples:["SBIN","BANKBARODA","CANBK","PNB","UNIONBANK","INDIANB","BANKINDIA","IOB","CENTRALBK","MAHABANK","UCOBANK","PSB"]},{name:"NBFCs & Lending",oneLiner:"Non-bank lenders — consumer finance, vehicle loans, gold loans, SME lending.",details:"NBFCs borrow from banks and the market and lend to customers who banks don't reach as well — two-wheeler finance, consumer-durable EMI, gold loans, SME loans, microfinance, vehicle finance. They tend to grow faster than banks but are more vulnerable in funding stress (when wholesale funding dries up). Key metrics: AUM growth, NIM, credit cost, and GNPA.",examples:["BAJFINANCE","BAJAJFINSV","CHOLAFIN","SUNDARMFIN","MUTHOOTFIN","SHRIRAMFIN","CREDITACC","BAJAJHLDNG","CHOLAHLDNG","ARMANFIN"]},{name:"Housing Finance",oneLiner:"Specialist home-loan lenders.",details:"HFCs focus specifically on residential mortgages. The business is simpler than a bank — borrow long, lend long, pocket the spread. The sector moves with housing demand, interest rates, and funding costs. Affordable housing has been a structural growth pocket. HFCs in India are regulated differently from banks but since 2019 have converged toward the same regulatory framework.",examples:["LICHSGFIN","PNBHOUSING","CANFINHOME","HOMEFIRST","AAVAS","APTUS","REPCOHOME","BAJAJHFL","SAMMAANCAP","INDIASHLTR"]},{name:"Insurance",oneLiner:"Life and general insurance — premiums in, claims out, investment income in between.",details:"Insurance companies collect premiums and invest the float until claims are paid. Life insurance is a long-term compounding business — APE (annual premium equivalent) growth, VNB (value of new business) margin, and persistency ratios are the key metrics. General insurance (health, motor, property) is more claims-ratio-driven and is seeing structural growth in health-insurance penetration.",examples:["SBILIFE","HDFCLIFE","ICICIPRULI","ICICIGI","LICI","STARHEALTH","NIACL","GICRE","GODIGIT","NIVABUPA"]},{name:"Capital Markets",oneLiner:"Brokers, asset managers, exchanges, depositories, and investment platforms.",details:"The plumbing of Indian capital markets — stock brokers (discount and full-service), asset management companies (mutual funds), the stock exchanges, depositories, and wealth managers. Revenue is directly levered to market activity: trading volumes, AUM growth, IPO issuances, retail participation. When markets rally, this industry is one of the steepest beneficiaries. When markets correct, the names correct first.",examples:["HDFCAMC","NUVAMA","MOTILALOFS","MCX","CDSL","BSE","CAMS","KFINTECH","UTIAMC","ABSLAMC","NAM-INDIA","ANANDRATHI","CRISIL","ICRA","CARERATING","360ONE","5PAISA","CHOICEIN"]}],siblingsTitle:"Momentum siblings",siblingsParas:["Financial Services siblings can be clustered into three groups. Banks (Private + PSU) tend to move together on credit-growth data, RBI rate decisions, and asset-quality disclosures. NBFCs and Housing Finance move in the same rate-and-credit cycle as banks but with more leverage — they tend to outperform in easy-money phases and underperform in tightening cycles. Capital Markets names have their own beat — they rally when the broader market rallies and retail activity rises.","Insurance has the weakest correlation with the rest of the sector — insurance is more of a long-term compounding story driven by premium growth and demographic spread rather than the rate cycle, though insurance stocks do move somewhat with bond yields (higher yields compress embedded-value math for life insurers)."],driversTitle:"What moves this sector",drivers:["RBI rate decisions and liquidity stance — the single biggest lever","Credit-growth data — monthly RBI releases, loan-book growth of listed names","Asset quality — GNPA, slippages, provisioning, write-offs","Government capex and private-sector borrowing — drives corporate-loan demand","Retail credit demand — home loans, personal loans, credit cards","Market cycle — directly drives broker and AMC revenue","Insurance premium growth — especially health and protection","Regulatory announcements — RBI, IRDAI, SEBI policy changes"],closingQuote:"Financial Services is the biggest sector by weight and the clearest read on the RBI cycle — every rate decision, every credit-growth datapoint shows up here first."},g={kicker:"KNOW YOUR SECTOR",headline:"Healthcare",tagline:"Pharma, hospitals, diagnostics — the business of keeping India healthy",overview:["Healthcare covers the companies that make medicines (pharma), run hospitals and diagnostic chains, and provide other healthcare services. Indian pharma is a global export powerhouse — the country supplies a huge share of the world's generic drugs, and many large companies earn 40-60% of their revenue from the US. At home, hospital chains and diagnostic labs have boomed on rising insurance coverage and lifestyle diseases.","The sector has two very different beats. Export pharma moves with US generic pricing, USFDA inspection outcomes, and product-launch calendars. Domestic pharma and hospitals move with price hikes, patient volumes, and private health-insurance growth. MNC pharma is a small, defensive, premium-valued subset. Diagnostics is a high-growth, high-ROCE, structurally expanding business.",e(3)],industries:[{name:"Pharma",oneLiner:"Indian pharma majors — generics, APIs, formulations, domestic and exports.",details:"Indian pharma makes both active pharmaceutical ingredients (APIs) and finished formulations (tablets, capsules, injectables). Exports — especially to the US — are the biggest revenue pool for large players. The US generic business is a tough game — pricing is falling 4-6% a year, but volume and new launches compensate. Domestic branded generics are higher-margin and more stable. USFDA inspections, product approvals, and patent expiries are the catalysts to track.",examples:["SUNPHARMA","DRREDDY","CIPLA","LUPIN","DIVISLAB","TORNTPHARM","ALKEM","AUROPHARMA","GLENMARK","JBCHEPHARM","MANKIND","AJANTPHARM","ERIS","IPCALAB","NEULANDLAB"]},{name:"Pharma - MNC",oneLiner:"India arms of global pharma multinationals — premium branded, mostly domestic.",details:"Indian subsidiaries of global pharma giants (Pfizer, Abbott, GSK, Sanofi and similar). They focus on in-licensed patented and premium branded products, serve mostly the Indian market, and pay out large dividends. They are less volatile than Indian exporters, valued at premium multiples, and tend to grow in line with Indian pharma market growth (8-12% annually). A classic defensive allocation.",examples:["PFIZER","SANOFI","GLAXO","ABBOTINDIA","ASTRAZEN","PGHL"]},{name:"Hospitals & Diagnostics",oneLiner:"Hospital chains, diagnostic labs, and healthcare-services companies.",details:"Hospital chains run multi-specialty hospitals, specialty centres (oncology, cardiology, IVF), and increasingly day-care. Revenue drivers are occupancy, ARPOB (average revenue per occupied bed), case mix (high-margin specialties), and insurance penetration. Diagnostic labs run pathology and imaging centres — a high-ROCE, high-growth category with organised players taking share from the unorganised. Both have benefitted enormously from rising health insurance and the post-COVID health consciousness.",examples:["APOLLOHOSP","MAXHEALTH","FORTIS","NH","RAINBOW","MEDANTA","ASTERDM","KIMS","METROPOLIS","LALPATHLAB","THYROCARE","KRSNAA","HCG","YATHARTH"]}],siblingsTitle:"Momentum siblings",siblingsParas:["Healthcare siblings are weaker than most sectors — Pharma and Hospitals often move independently. Within Pharma, the export-heavy names move together on US pricing data and USFDA news; MNC Pharma moves more with domestic pharma growth and currency. Hospitals & Diagnostics tends to move as a distinct pack with insurance-penetration data and occupancy numbers, occasionally pulling along with the broader Consumer Staples defensive rotation.","Specialty Chemicals in the Chemicals sector has some overlap too — many API intermediates are made by specialty chemical companies, so a strong global pharma API cycle can lift names in both sectors."],driversTitle:"What moves this sector",drivers:["US generic pricing environment and FDA inspection outcomes","New product launches and ANDA approvals by the USFDA","Indian pharma market growth and price-hike announcements","Health-insurance penetration — drives hospital and diagnostic volumes","Currency — rupee depreciation is a tailwind for exports","API and raw-material pricing — many now sourced from China","Patent expiries and specialty-drug opportunities (GLP-1, complex generics)","Regulatory tightening — DPCO pricing, NLEM revisions"],closingQuote:"Healthcare in India is split across three very different engines — US generics, domestic branded pharma, and hospitals — and they rarely fire at the same time."},y={kicker:"KNOW YOUR SECTOR",headline:"Information Technology",tagline:"Indian IT services, product companies, EdTech, and internet businesses",overview:["Information Technology is dominated by Indian IT services — the world's back-office for enterprise software development, system integration, and business process services. TCS, Infosys, Wipro, HCL and their peers export to US and European enterprises; IT services alone contributes over $200bn in annual exports. Around the services core, a growing cluster of IT products, EdTech, IT hardware, and internet/e-commerce companies has listed in the last five years.","The sector's rhythm depends on who you are. Services majors move with global enterprise tech spend (heavily US BFSI-weighted), USD-INR, and the AI disruption narrative. Internet and e-commerce companies move with growth metrics (MAUs, GMV, take-rates). EdTech has had a cycle of boom-and-bust. IT products and hardware are growing faster than services but from a smaller base.",e(5)],industries:[{name:"IT Services",oneLiner:"India's global enterprise tech back-office — TCS, Infosys, Wipro and peers.",details:"These are the companies that build and maintain software, run infrastructure, and provide digital transformation for global enterprises. The biggest customer pool is US banks and financial services, followed by retail, manufacturing, and healthcare. Revenue is in dollars (rupee depreciation = tailwind), delivery is mostly from India (cost advantage). The hot current debate is how AI changes the revenue-per-employee equation — that's the single biggest narrative driver right now. Growth cycles follow global enterprise tech budgets.",examples:["TCS","INFY","HCLTECH","WIPRO","TECHM","LTM","PERSISTENT","COFORGE","MPHASIS","LTTS","TATAELXSI","CYIENT","MASTEK","ECLERX"]},{name:"IT Products",oneLiner:"Software products and SaaS — smaller, product-IP-led companies.",details:"These are the Indian companies that build and license their own software products rather than providing custom services. SaaS (software-as-a-service) models, payment infrastructure, enterprise software, security tools. Higher gross margins than services (because the product scales), but smaller revenue base. The listed universe is still thin; many leading Indian SaaS names remain private.",examples:["OFSS","INTELLECT","NEWGEN","RATEGAIN","TANLA","NUCLEUS","RAMCOSYS","EMUDHRA","IRIS","QUICKHEAL"]},{name:"IT Hardware",oneLiner:"Servers, laptops, networking gear, and IT infrastructure hardware.",details:"Companies that sell and distribute IT hardware — servers, laptops, desktops, networking equipment, data-centre hardware. Some are pure distributors; some manufacture or assemble. Growth is tied to enterprise IT capex, data-centre buildouts (a booming theme thanks to AI and hyperscalers), and domestic PC/laptop demand. Margins are thin, volume-driven.",examples:["NETWEB","HCL-INSYS","TVSELECT","CONTROLPR","SMARTLINK","MOSCHIP"]},{name:"Internet & E-commerce",oneLiner:"Consumer internet, quick-commerce, food delivery, fintech, and marketplaces.",details:'The "new-economy" listed names — food delivery, quick-commerce grocery, travel bookings, online marketplaces, digital payments, beauty and fashion e-commerce. They are mostly high-growth, loss-making-to-barely-profitable, and their stock moves are driven by growth metrics (order growth, GMV, take-rate expansion) and the path to profitability narrative. Valuation is done differently — revenue multiples, embedded-value math, and unit-economics.',examples:["ETERNAL","SWIGGY","NYKAA","PAYTM","POLICYBZR","INDIAMART","NAUKRI","JUSTDIAL","MAPMYINDIA","AFFLE","ROUTE","NAZARA","IXIGO","TBOTEK"]},{name:"EdTech",oneLiner:"Online education platforms and test-preparation companies.",details:"Companies that offer test preparation (JEE, NEET, CAT), online schooling, upskilling, and professional certification via digital platforms. The sector boomed in COVID then corrected sharply as students returned to classrooms. The listed universe is small; much of the space remains private. Revenue drivers are student enrolments, course pricing, and retention — operating leverage is high but content and marketing costs eat into it.",examples:["NIITLTD","NIITMTS","APTECHT","COMPUSOFT","ZEELEARN"]}],siblingsTitle:"Momentum siblings",siblingsParas:["IT Services is the anchor — when the tier-1 names (TCS, Infosys, Wipro) move on quarterly results and forward guidance, mid-tier services (Coforge, Persistent, Mphasis, LTIM) typically follow with higher beta. IT Products often moves somewhat independently since the names are small and news-driven, but AI adoption and digital transformation narratives lift both Services and Products together.","Internet & E-commerce is a separate beat — these names move on user-growth metrics, path-to-profitability news, and the broader risk-on/risk-off mood. They have almost no correlation with classical IT services in the short term. EdTech and IT Hardware are both niche and news-driven; their siblings effect within the sector is limited."],driversTitle:"What moves this sector",drivers:["US enterprise tech spending — especially BFSI, retail, manufacturing","USD-INR — every 1% rupee depreciation adds roughly 30-40 bps to operating margin","AI disruption narrative — reshapes revenue-per-employee and pricing models","US Fed rate cycle — rate cuts revive enterprise tech budgets","Data-centre capex and hyperscaler spending — lifts IT hardware","Internet user-growth metrics, GMV growth, take-rate data","Indian consumer internet regulation — RBI, MeitY policy changes","Discretionary-demand tech budgets versus must-have tech spend"],closingQuote:"IT is India's largest export earner and the closest read on global enterprise tech — every US Fed move, every AI narrative shift, every Fortune 500 budget cycle shows up here."},b={kicker:"KNOW YOUR SECTOR",headline:"Materials",tagline:"Metals, cement, paints, and the physical stuff that builds everything",overview:["Materials is the sector that supplies the raw and semi-finished physical inputs for construction, manufacturing, consumer products, and infrastructure. Steel, aluminium, cement, paints, tiles, paper, plastics, building products — all the stuff that ends up inside a building, a vehicle, a fridge, or a package. The sector is highly cyclical, capital-intensive, and sensitive to commodity prices.","There are three sub-rhythms inside Materials. Base metals and mining move with global commodity prices (steel, aluminium, copper). Cement and building materials move with domestic construction — government infra, real-estate, housing. Paints, plastics, and finished building products move more with consumer demand and rising product penetration. The sector often leads the real economy — commodity-price turns show up in Materials stocks before they show up in anyone's earnings.",e(9)],industries:[{name:"Cement",oneLiner:"India's cement makers — the binder of every construction project.",details:"Cement companies produce clinker (from limestone) and grind it into cement, which is used for concrete in every construction project. Demand drivers are housing, infrastructure, industrial, and commercial construction — roughly 65% housing, 20% infra, 15% other. Pricing is regional (freight costs are high), and the industry has consolidated heavily (UltraTech, Ambuja, Shree). Margins swing with fuel and freight (pet coke, coal, diesel), demand seasonality, and pricing discipline.",examples:["ULTRACEMCO","AMBUJACEM","ACC","SHREECEM","JKCEMENT","DALBHARAT","RAMCOCEM","BIRLACORPN","HEIDELBERG","STARCEMENT","INDIACEM","JKLAKSHMI","NUVOCO","JSWCEMENT"]},{name:"Steel",oneLiner:"Steel producers — integrated mills, flat products, long products.",details:"Steel makers convert iron ore into finished steel — flat products (sheets, coils for autos, appliances, construction) and long products (rods, beams for construction). Integrated mills mine ore, make iron, and roll steel; standalone mills buy feedstock. Steel earnings are highly cyclical — global steel spreads, Chinese policy (export taxes, production curbs), iron ore and coking coal prices all swing quarterly results. It's one of the most geared bets on the global industrial cycle.",examples:["JSWSTEEL","JINDALSTEL","JSL","SHYAMMETL","GOODLUCK","SARDAEN","RAJRATAN","BANSALWIRE","USHAMART","GALLANTT"]},{name:"Mining & Metals",oneLiner:"Base-metal miners, smelters, and integrated metal majors.",details:"Mining and base-metal companies — copper, zinc, lead, nickel, and iron-ore operations. The industry is small in India compared to steel and cement but contains a few global-scale names (especially in zinc). Revenue and margin are directly tied to LME prices. Mining lease auctions and royalty changes affect economics. Power costs (smelters are very power-heavy) matter a lot.",examples:["VEDL","HINDZINC","COALINDIA","NMDC","HINDCOPPER","MOIL","GMDCLTD","KIOCL","IMFA","GRAVITA","RHIM","VESUVIUS"]},{name:"Aluminium",oneLiner:"Aluminium smelters and integrated aluminium majors.",details:"Companies that mine bauxite, refine it into alumina, and smelt alumina into aluminium metal. Aluminium is power-intensive (a smelter eats 13-15 MWh per tonne), so domestic captive power advantage is huge. Demand drivers are transport (auto, aerospace, transmission conductors), packaging, and construction. Global LME aluminium prices set the revenue; power tariff and alumina cost set the margin.",examples:["HINDALCO","NATIONALUM","MAANALU","ARFIN"]},{name:"Paints & Coatings",oneLiner:"Decorative paints, industrial coatings, and the paint industry ecosystem.",details:"Indian paints is one of the highest-quality industries in the market — high margins, strong brands, pricing power, asset-light distribution. Demand is 75% decorative (homes, repaint) and 25% industrial (auto, coatings). Decorative paint has a 4-5 year repaint cycle and grows roughly 1.5x GDP. Margins are sensitive to crude-oil-linked inputs (titanium dioxide, monomers). Competitive dynamics shifted in 2024 with new entrants — watch market-share trends.",examples:["ASIANPAINT","BERGEPAINT","KANSAINER","AKZOINDIA","INDIGOPNTS","SHALPAINTS"]},{name:"Building Materials",oneLiner:"Pipes, sanitaryware, fittings, and home-building products.",details:"This industry covers a range of discrete home-construction products — plastic pipes (agricultural, plumbing, fire-safety), sanitaryware (toilets, bath fittings), plywood and laminates, MDF, water-proofing chemicals. Highly cyclical to real-estate construction but with a strong aftermarket refurbishment component. Brand power and distribution depth distinguish winners.",examples:["CENTURYPLY","GREENPLY","GREENLAM","GREENPANEL","STYLAMIND","RUSHIL","ARCHIDPLY","AIROLAM"]},{name:"Ceramics & Building Products",oneLiner:"Tiles, sanitaryware, ceramic products, and decorative home surfaces.",details:"The ceramics subset of building materials — floor and wall tiles, vitrified tiles, sanitaryware, bathware. Heavy concentration in Morbi (Gujarat) ceramic cluster. Demand is 70% real-estate linked and 30% refurbishment. Gas prices are a huge cost driver (kilns run on natural gas). Export opportunity (especially to US and the Middle East) has become a structural tailwind for the organised producers.",examples:["KAJARIACER","CERA","ASAHIINDIA","SOMANYCERA","ORIENTBELL","BORORENEW","BOROLTD","LAOPALA","ASIANTILES"]},{name:"Paper & Packaging",oneLiner:"Paper producers, packaging materials, and industrial packaging.",details:"Paper companies make writing/printing paper, packaging paper (kraft, corrugated), tissue, and speciality paper. E-commerce and FMCG packaging have been structural tailwinds for corrugated and kraft; writing paper has faced digitisation headwinds. Pulp prices, wastepaper availability, and energy costs are the big input swings. Packaging as a broader theme overlaps with the Industrials sector's Packaging industry.",examples:["JKPAPER","WSTCSTPAPR","TNPL","ANDHRAPAP","SESHAPAPER","ORIENTPPR","EMAMIPAP","SATIA","PAKKA","STARPAPER"]},{name:"Plastics",oneLiner:"Commodity plastics, engineered polymers, and plastic-product makers.",details:"Plastics covers everything from polymer resin (PE, PP, PVC) to finished plastic products (pipes, containers, auto parts, appliance parts, films). Upstream plastic resin prices move with crude oil; downstream product margins depend on how fast they can pass through. Consumer-packaging plastics, auto plastics, and agricultural plastics all have distinct demand cycles.",examples:["SUPREMEIND","ASTRAL","APOLLOPIPE","PRINCEPIPE","FINPIPE","NILKAMAL","SAFARI","VIPIND","TIMETECHNO","KINGFA","SHAILY","JAICORPLTD"]}],siblingsTitle:"Momentum siblings",siblingsParas:["Materials has three natural clusters. Steel, Mining & Metals, and Aluminium move together as the commodity-metals pack — global LME prices, Chinese policy, and global manufacturing PMI pull them in the same direction. Cement, Building Materials, Ceramics & Building Products, Paints & Coatings, and Plastics (domestic pipes, containers) form the domestic-construction pack — real-estate demand and government infra pull them together. Paper & Packaging has its own beat tied to pulp prices and FMCG demand.","The cross-sector siblings are worth calling out too — when Cement and Building Materials move, Real Estate (in Construction & Real Estate) and Cables & Wires (in Industrials) are usually moving in parallel. That's the classic housing cycle footprint."],driversTitle:"What moves this sector",drivers:["Global commodity prices — LME steel, aluminium, copper, zinc, coking coal","China policy — production curbs and export taxes swing metal prices globally","Indian real-estate construction cycle and government infra capex","Crude oil and natural gas — inputs to paints, plastics, ceramics kilns","Coal and pet coke — cement fuel costs","Iron-ore auction outcomes and mining regulations","New capacity additions and pricing discipline within each industry","Export demand from US, EU, and Middle East for building products"],closingQuote:"Materials leads the real economy in both directions — commodity prices turn before earnings turn, and the equity often moves before the price does."},f={kicker:"KNOW YOUR SECTOR",headline:"Media & Telecom",tagline:"Mobile networks, broadcasters, streaming, and telecom infrastructure",overview:["Media & Telecom covers the companies that connect India digitally — mobile-network operators, broadcasters, streaming platforms, film/content producers, and telecom equipment and tower companies. Post the 2016 Jio disruption, the sector consolidated from 12+ operators to effectively three — Reliance Jio, Bharti Airtel, and Vodafone-Idea. That consolidation has finally allowed pricing to start inching up, reviving telecom profitability.","Media has been the trickier half of this sector. Traditional TV broadcasting has struggled with OTT disruption. Film production is highly hit-or-miss. Streaming is largely private. The listed media universe is smaller and more news-driven than telecom. Telecom infrastructure (towers) has moved to private entities (Indus Towers) and data-centre plays.",e(3)],industries:[{name:"Telecom",oneLiner:"Mobile-network operators and large telecom services providers.",details:"The wireless service providers — the companies you pay your monthly mobile bill to. After Jio's disruptive entry in 2016, the Indian market consolidated into a three-operator market (Jio, Airtel, Vodafone-Idea). ARPU (average revenue per user) is the single most important metric — every 10 rupee ARPU hike flows to the bottom line. 5G rollout capex and tariff-hike timing are the key catalysts. Vodafone-Idea's survival/capex is the wild card.",examples:["BHARTIARTL","BHARTIHEXA","TATACOMM","IDEA","TTML","MTNL","RCOM"]},{name:"Telecom Equipment",oneLiner:"Network equipment makers, tower companies, and telecom infrastructure.",details:"Companies that supply hardware and infrastructure to telecom operators — tower companies, fibre operators, network-equipment and optical-component makers, small-cell and Wi-Fi equipment. The 5G buildout and fibre deepening have been the structural tailwinds. Tower and passive-infra companies earn steady rental income from operators.",examples:["TEJASNET","UMIYA-MRO"]},{name:"Media & Entertainment",oneLiner:"Broadcasters, film producers, music labels, and content creators.",details:"Media is a mixed universe — large broadcasters (Zee, Sun TV), film production houses (PVR-Inox, EROS), music and radio, and news broadcasting. Revenue sources are advertising (cyclical, tied to GDP growth), subscription (DTH, cable, OTT), and theatrical/content (film box-office, content sales). OTT disruption has pressured TV broadcasters; cinema chains rely on big hit films. Regulatory changes (NTO, new regulations) can reshape economics.",examples:["PVRINOX","SUNTV","ZEEL","SAREGAMA","TIPSMUSIC","TIPSFILMS","DBCORP","ENIL","TVTODAY","NDTV","SHEMAROO","WONDERLA","INDUSTOWER","HFCL"]}],siblingsTitle:"Momentum siblings",siblingsParas:["Telecom and Telecom Equipment move together during a capex-heavy phase — 5G rollouts, fibre buildouts, and data-centre expansions lift both operators and equipment/tower names. Tariff-hike news lifts Telecom operators alone and rarely extends to equipment.","Media & Entertainment has weak sibling links within this sector. It tends to correlate more with Consumer Discretionary (film demand, advertising spend) and with GDP growth (ad-revenue proxy) than with Telecom. Expect Media & Entertainment to move on its own catalysts."],driversTitle:"What moves this sector",drivers:["Tariff hikes (ARPU) — the single biggest telecom earnings lever","Spectrum auctions and capex cycles — affects both operators and equipment","5G rollout pace and monetisation — structural for the next 3-5 years","Subscriber addition and churn data — monthly TRAI reports","Data-consumption growth — drives equipment and tower demand","Advertising spend cycle — drives TV broadcasters and film revenue","Regulatory changes — TRAI, MIB policy shifts","OTT subscription growth and film box-office trends"],closingQuote:"After a decade of value destruction, Indian telecom has finally returned to pricing power — the next tariff cycle is the most important call the sector makes."},v={kicker:"KNOW YOUR SECTOR",headline:"Textiles",tagline:"Yarn, fabric, garments, and the home-furnishings India exports",overview:["Textiles is one of India's oldest industrial sectors and remains a major employer and exporter. The sector spans the full value chain — cotton and synthetic yarn, woven and knit fabric, fabric processing (dyeing, printing), garments (apparel), and home/technical textiles (bedsheets, towels, industrial fabrics). India is one of the world's largest cotton producers and yarn exporters.","The sector's stock-market rhythm is deeply tied to global export demand — US and EU retailer orders, China+1 sourcing shifts, and the competitiveness advantage of Bangladesh and Vietnam. Domestic branded apparel (in Consumer Discretionary) is a separate beat; the Textiles sector proper is more B2B and export-heavy. Cotton prices, currency, and power/labour costs are the biggest margin swings.",e(4)],industries:[{name:"Textile - Apparel & Brands",oneLiner:"Branded apparel makers and vertically integrated garment houses.",details:"Companies that make branded garments — both domestic brands and export-garmenting for global retailers (Walmart, H&M, Gap, Target). Some are vertically integrated (yarn → fabric → garment); some specialise at garment level. Revenue drivers are export orders, fashion-cycle trends, and domestic brand expansion. Margins depend on cotton prices, labour costs, and order-book visibility.",examples:["MONTECARLO","CANTABIL","GOCOLORS","WELSPUNLIV","HIMATSEIDE","INDTERRAIN","RAYMONDLSL","JINDALPOLY","MALLCOM","GARFIBRES","VIPCLOTHNG","SPAL","SBC"]},{name:"Textile - Fabric & Processing",oneLiner:"Woven and knit fabric makers, dyeing and processing units.",details:"Companies that weave or knit fabric from yarn, and companies that process fabric (dyeing, printing, finishing). They sit in the middle of the value chain, supplying to garment makers and home-furnishings companies. Heavy dependence on cotton and polyester prices, water and power costs, and environmental-compliance investment. Margins are thinner than branded apparel but volumes are higher.",examples:["LUXIND","SIYSIL","DONEAR","BSL","RUBYMILLS","ALOKINDS","WEIZMANIND","ORBTEXP","AYMSYNTEX"]},{name:"Textile - Home & Technical",oneLiner:"Bedsheets, towels, rugs, and industrial/technical fabrics.",details:"This industry has two distinct sub-pockets. Home textiles — bedsheets, towels, rugs — are largely export-driven, with US big-box retailers being the biggest customer. Technical textiles — industrial fabrics, agro-textiles, geo-textiles, medical textiles — is a higher-margin growth pocket supported by government PLI schemes. Cotton prices, US demand, and retail-inventory cycles matter.",examples:["CHEVIOT","GLOSTERLTD","WAKEFIT"]},{name:"Textile - Yarn & Fibre",oneLiner:"Cotton and synthetic yarn spinners.",details:"The upstream of textiles — spinners who turn raw cotton and synthetic fibres into yarn, which is then sold to fabric and garment makers. Highly commodity-like — yarn prices move with cotton prices and global yarn demand. Indian yarn is very exposed to China and Bangladesh demand. Power costs and cotton availability are the big operating swings. Margins are thin and cyclical.",examples:["GRASIM","ARVIND","RSWM","BANSWRAS","NAHARSPING","SANATHAN","SANGAMIND","SPORTKING","BOMDYEING","NITINSPIN","CENTENKA","NAHARINDUS"]}],siblingsTitle:"Momentum siblings",siblingsParas:["Textiles has a clean upstream-to-downstream flow. Yarn & Fibre moves first on cotton-price and global-yarn-demand data. A few weeks or quarters later, Fabric & Processing sees the knock-on effect. Apparel & Brands sees it last as garment orders translate into shipped volumes. In a down-cycle the same order holds — yarn price cracks first, fabric margins compress next, garment order-books slow last.","Home & Technical Textiles has a somewhat independent rhythm tied to US retailer inventory and the structural technical-textile PLI theme. It can outperform or underperform the rest of the sector depending on global retailer de-stocking/re-stocking."],driversTitle:"What moves this sector",drivers:["Global cotton prices and Indian cotton crop quality","US and EU retailer inventory cycles — destocking vs restocking phases","China+1 sourcing shifts — structural tailwind for Indian textiles","USD-INR — rupee depreciation helps export-heavy names","Power and labour costs — critical for spinning and processing","PLI scheme disbursements — especially for technical textiles and MMF","Competitive pressure from Bangladesh, Vietnam, and China","Duty structures and FTA negotiations — UK, EU, GCC trade deals"],closingQuote:"Textiles is one of India's oldest export engines — when US retailers restock and cotton prices cooperate, the whole value chain moves together."},w={kicker:"KNOW YOUR SECTOR",headline:"Transportation",tagline:"Moving people, goods, and cargo across the country and the world",overview:["Transportation is the sector that moves stuff — airlines flying passengers, logistics companies trucking goods, shipping lines carrying containers across oceans, and tourism/travel companies organising holidays and corporate trips. Each industry here has its own cycle, costs, and competitive dynamics, but all share an exposure to fuel prices, macro demand, and capacity-utilisation economics.","Post-COVID, the sector has had a dramatic rebound — air passenger traffic has crossed pre-COVID peaks, e-commerce has pushed logistics volumes to record levels, and international shipping had a historic boom-and-bust in 2021-2023. Fuel costs, capacity discipline, consolidation, and demand cycles are the four things that shape every industry here.",e(4)],industries:[{name:"Airlines",oneLiner:"Passenger airlines and commercial aviation.",details:"Airlines are one of the hardest industries in the world to run profitably — fuel is a huge cost (ATF ≈ 40% of opex), competition is fierce, and capital intensity is high. Indian aviation has consolidated around three groups (IndiGo, Tata/Air India/Vistara, Akasa/others). Load factor, yield (per-km revenue), fuel cost, and fleet utilisation are the four numbers that matter. Every rupee move in ATF and every percentage point in load factor swings quarterly results.",examples:["INDIGO","GLOBALVECT"]},{name:"Logistics",oneLiner:"Trucking, warehousing, express delivery, and supply-chain services.",details:"Logistics companies move goods between factories, warehouses, retailers, and consumers. The sector is fragmented but consolidating fast. E-commerce and quick-commerce have driven explosive growth in express parcel and warehousing. Rail logistics, container logistics, and 3PL (third-party logistics) are other growth pockets. Revenue grows with GST-driven formalisation, e-commerce volumes, and warehousing network expansion. Fuel costs and route density are the biggest operating swings.",examples:["ADANIPORTS","CONCOR","DELHIVERY","BLUEDART","JSWINFRA","TCI","TCIEXP","MAHLOG","RAILTEL","VRLLOG","TVSSCS","GPPL","SHADOWFAX"]},{name:"Shipping",oneLiner:"Ocean shipping companies — dry-bulk, tankers, containers, LNG.",details:"Companies that operate ships moving cargo on international and domestic sea routes. Indian listed shipping is dominated by a few names across dry-bulk (ore, coal, grain), tankers (crude, product), and shipping services. Rates are globally set and highly cyclical — the Baltic Dry Index and crude-tanker rates swing dramatically. 2021-2023 was a historic freight-rate boom; normalisation followed. Global trade volumes, fleet supply, and geopolitical routes (Red Sea, Suez, Panama) are the drivers.",examples:["GESHIP","SCI","SHREEJISPG","TRANSWORLD","ESSARSHPNG"]},{name:"Travel & Tourism",oneLiner:"Online travel booking, tour operators, and leisure-tourism companies.",details:"Travel booking platforms (flight, hotel, holiday), corporate-travel companies, and tour operators. Revenue is largely transaction-based (take-rate on bookings). Post-COVID, domestic leisure and corporate travel have boomed; international travel has also rebounded. Revenue swings with travel demand, airline/hotel capacity, and market share. Competition from direct-booking channels and aggregators is the key risk.",examples:["IRCTC"]}],siblingsTitle:"Momentum siblings",siblingsParas:["Airlines and Travel & Tourism move in tandem — a strong travel demand cycle lifts both passenger load factors and booking-platform revenue. Logistics has its own beat tied to manufacturing and e-commerce rather than travel — it correlates more with the consumer-demand and industrial-activity cycle than with airlines.","Shipping is the biggest outlier — it moves on global freight rates and Baltic indices that have almost no connection to domestic airline, logistics, or travel demand. A shipping rate boom can happen while domestic logistics is dull, and vice versa."],driversTitle:"What moves this sector",drivers:["Fuel prices — ATF for airlines, diesel for logistics, bunker for shipping","Air passenger traffic — monthly DGCA reports","E-commerce order volumes — drive express logistics demand","Global freight rates — Baltic indices, container rates, tanker rates","Travel demand — domestic leisure, corporate, international, wedding season","Capacity additions and consolidation — affect pricing power","Geopolitical routes — Red Sea, Suez disruptions reshape shipping","GST-driven formalisation and warehousing network expansion"],closingQuote:"Transportation is cyclical, capital-intensive, and operationally hard — but when a cycle turns, the operating leverage here is among the highest in the market."},A={kicker:"KNOW YOUR SECTOR",headline:"Miscellaneous",tagline:"Conglomerates and diversified businesses that don't fit one box",overview:["Miscellaneous is a catch-all classification for companies whose business spans multiple unrelated sectors — conglomerates with interests across many industries, diversified holding companies, and niche businesses like printing and stationery that don't fit cleanly elsewhere. It is not a thematic sector and does not move as a single economic entity.","Because the companies here span multiple businesses, their stocks respond to a mix of drivers — each conglomerate's component-business cycle matters. There is no single macro lever that moves this sector as a whole.",e(3)],industries:[{name:"Conglomerates",oneLiner:"Large diversified groups with businesses across unrelated sectors.",details:"These are the Indian conglomerate holding structures — groups with interests spanning infrastructure, energy, retail, media, metals, and more. Revenue and earnings are a sum-of-parts story: each underlying business has its own dynamics, and the holding company's stock reflects a blend of all of them plus a conglomerate discount (or premium). Flagship-business momentum, capex announcements, and restructurings are the key catalysts.",examples:["3MINDIA","DCMSHRIRAM","NAVA","QUESS","BALMLAWRIE","ASPINWALL","TTKHLTCARE","GOCLCORP","INDOFARM","BLUSPRING"]},{name:"Diversified",oneLiner:"Companies with multiple business lines that don't anchor to a single sector.",details:"Companies that operate across two or more sectors without clearly being one thing. They may be smaller than conglomerates but share the same sum-of-parts analysis challenge. Each component business has its own cycle — investors must track each one and weigh them.",examples:["MFSL","SIS","RITES","TEAMLEASE","CMSINFO","MEDIASSIST","KAPSTON","SIGNPOST","SANGHVIMOV","RAIN","CROWN","SEAMECLTD","DREDGECORP","UDS","INOXGREEN","ALPHAGEO"]},{name:"Printing & Stationery",oneLiner:"Pens, notebooks, commercial printing, and stationery products.",details:"A small, niche industry — pen makers, notebook manufacturers, commercial printing companies, school-stationery brands. Growth is tied to back-to-school demand, educational-enrolment trends, and premiumisation (higher-priced gel pens, branded notebooks). Margins are stable but growth is limited.",examples:["DOMS","FLAIR","LINC","KOKUYOCMLN","SCHAND","NAVNETEDUL","REPRO"]}],siblingsTitle:"Momentum siblings",siblingsParas:["Miscellaneous does not have classical sibling rotation because the industries inside it are unrelated. Each conglomerate or diversified company responds to its own largest business lines. Printing & Stationery has no sector siblings of its own — it trades on its own narrow drivers.","Instead, watch for siblings outside this sector. A conglomerate's key exposure (say, to metals or infra) links it to the momentum of that specific sector — so you may see an Adani name move alongside Infrastructure & Construction, or an ITC move alongside FMCG - Diversified."],driversTitle:"What moves this sector",drivers:["Underlying business-segment performance of each conglomerate","Capex announcements and project commissioning","Restructurings, demergers, and holding-company unlock events","Flagship-business sector cycle (e.g. metals, infra, retail, energy)","Back-to-school demand — especially for stationery","Tax and policy changes affecting conglomerate structures","Promoter-group actions, buyouts, and capital allocation","Currency and raw-material swings in each underlying business"],closingQuote:"Miscellaneous is not a sector so much as a label — the stocks here live or die by their largest business line, not by any shared theme."},I={Industrials:r,"Aerospace & Defence":o,Automobile:l,Chemicals:c,"Construction & Real Estate":d,"Consumer Discretionary":m,"Consumer Staples":h,Energy:u,"Financial Services":p,Healthcare:g,"Information Technology":y,Materials:b,"Media & Telecom":f,Textiles:v,Transportation:w,Miscellaneous:A};function T(a){for(const[i,t]of Object.entries(I)){const s=t.industries.find(n=>n.name===a);if(s)return{industry:s,sector:i}}}export{I as S,T as g};
